## 💭 Reflection

This day changed how I think about model evaluation compared to the supervised workflow from Week 4. There's no target column to tell me whether a customer was assigned to the "correct" segment — instead I had to combine quantitative evidence (inertia, silhouette) with judgment about what the discovered groups actually represent. The biggest practical lesson was that scaling is not optional here: K-Means is entirely distance-based, so leaving Income on a raw 15–137 scale next to Age would have let it dominate the clustering.

## ✅ Conclusion

Today I learned the core unsupervised learning workflow: scale first, use the elbow method to narrow candidate `k` values, confirm with silhouette score, then interpret the final clusters using their real-world feature means rather than the plot alone. I'm carrying forward the same standard I set in Week 4 — documenting *why* a choice was made, not just what the choice was.
